.TH BIRD-MANAGER 8 "@@MONTH@@ @@YEAR@@" "bird-manager @@VERSION@@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bird-manager \- Web dashboard for BIRD configuration management
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B systemctl
.RB [ start | stop | status | restart ]
.br
.B /etc/bird-manager/.env
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bird-manager
is a web application for managing BIRD networks, ASN:COMM communities and
BGP peers. SQLite is the only and enforced data source for the BIRD daemon. The application is a plain WSGI
application served by
.BR waitress (8)
on port 5000.
.PP
The generated BIRD configuration is split into files. The editable base
contains the router id and operator comments. Generated files contain routes,
protocols and community policies. The web panel exposes them as read-only
views or summary counters:
.TP
.I bird-manager.conf
Generated ordered include list. It is shown read-only in the web panel.
.TP
.I networks.conf
Editable base configuration. In a reset installation it contains only the
header and router id.
.TP
.I routes.conf
Generated static routes and peer reachability routes.
.TP
.I core.conf
Generated device, direct and BGP protocols.
.TP
.I policy_<id>.conf
Generated community definition and export policy for one community database ID.
.PP
The service reconciles the generated files at startup and after every database
mutation. It validates the complete included bundle with
.BR "bird -p" ,
updates
.I /etc/bird/live/bird-manager.conf
without modifying the main BIRD configuration, and reloads BIRD with
.BR "birdc configure" .
A reload failure leaves the web service running with a degraded health status.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is read from
.I .env
next to the package and, for a Debian installation, from
.IR /etc/bird-manager/.env .
Restart the service after editing it:
.B systemctl restart bird-manager
.TP
.B DB_PATH
SQLite database path. Package default:
.IR /var/lib/bird-manager/app.db .
.TP
.B BIRD_LIVE_DIR
Live directory read by BIRD. Package default:
.IR /etc/bird/live .
.TP
.B BIRD_STAGED_DIR
Generated staging directory. Package default:
.IR /var/lib/bird-manager/staged .
.TP
.B BIRD_MAIN_CONFIG
Read-only main BIRD configuration containing the stable manager include. Default:
.IR /etc/bird/bird.conf .
.TP
.B BIRD_MANIFEST_PATH
Manifest of generated files. Default:
.IR /var/lib/bird-manager/managed-files.json .
.TP
.B BIRD_PUBLISH_BAK_DIR
Directory with the rollback snapshot of the live bundle. Snapshots are kept
outside the BIRD include directory, so the live directory only contains files
that BIRD reads. Default:
.IR /var/lib/bird-manager/live-bak .
.TP
.B BIRD_BIN_PATH
BIRD binary used for parse validation. Default:
.IR bird .
.TP
.B BIRDC_BIN_PATH
BIRD control binary used for reload. Default:
.IR birdc .
.TP
.B BIRD_SOCKET
BIRD control socket. Empty means the daemon default,
.IR /run/bird/bird.ctl .
.TP
.B BIRD_ROUTER_ID
Router ID. The default,
.BR auto ,
detects a host IPv4 address and falls back to 192.0.2.1.
.TP
.B BIRD_LOCAL_AS
Local ASN. Default: 65500.
.TP
.B BIRD_BACKUP_RETENTION
Number of local full snapshot slots retained. The System tab shows used/limit
capacity. Valid range: 1 through 99. Default: 5.
.TP
.B USERNAME
Identifier for user's actions in journal and audit. Does not affect
permissions/access. Default:
.BR User .
.TP
.B APP_HOST
HTTP bind address. Use 127.0.0.1 when a reverse proxy is the only intended
entry point. Default:
.BR 0.0.0.0 .
.TP
.B BIRD_TRUSTED_SOURCES
Comma separated CIDR list of clients allowed to reach the API. The API has no
authentication, so this allowlist is the only gate in front of the BIRD
configuration; requests outside it are rejected with 403 before the body is
read. The token
.B auto
resolves to the network of this host's primary IPv4 interface; bridge subnets
are never added automatically. Default:
.BR "auto,127.0.0.1/32,::1/128" .
.TP
.B BIRD_TRUSTED_PROXIES
Comma separated CIDR list of reverse proxies whose
.B X-Forwarded-For
header is honoured. When empty the header is ignored, so a client cannot
present a fake address. Default: empty.
.TP
.B BIRD_TRUSTED_ORIGINS
Comma separated list of origins allowed to send POST/PUT/DELETE. When set, a
mutating request without a matching Origin header is rejected with 403, which
blocks cross-site browser requests; note that curl and scripts send no Origin.
When empty the check is disabled. Default: empty.
.TP
.B BIRD_MAX_BODY_BYTES
Largest accepted request body. Larger requests are rejected with 413 before
the body is read. Default: 1048576.
.TP
.B BIRD_DEBUG_ERRORS
When 1, 5xx responses carry the exception text. Keep 0 in production; the
traceback is always written to the journal. Default: 0.
.SH ACCESS CONTROL
Every response carries X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options,
Referrer-Policy, a strict Content-Security-Policy and Cache-Control: no-store.
The dashboard ships no inline script so the policy needs no unsafe-inline.
.PP
The effective policy is printed at startup:
.PP
.nf
[ACCESS] trusted sources: ...
.fi
.PP
Behind a reverse proxy set APP_HOST=127.0.0.1, add the proxy address to
BIRD_TRUSTED_PROXIES and the public origin to BIRD_TRUSTED_ORIGINS; see
README.md for a complete nginx example.
.SH GENERATED FILES
The main configuration contains one stable include:
.PP
.nf
include "/etc/bird/live/bird-manager.conf";
.fi
.PP
The generated entrypoint is ordered as:
.PP
.nf
include "/etc/bird/live/networks.conf";
include "/etc/bird/live/policy_<id>.conf";
include "/etc/bird/live/export.conf";
include "/etc/bird/live/routes.conf";
include "/etc/bird/live/core.conf";
.fi
.PP
A BIRD channel keeps exactly one export filter and a repeated
.B export filter
line silently overwrites the previous one, so the bundle always emits a single
.B export filter export_own_networks;
reference. The filter in export.conf tags every DB network with all of its
Communities, which means adding a Community no longer hides the Communities
exported before it; routes that are not in the database are not exported.
.PP
Apply never modifies the main configuration. Disabling a
community removes its policy file and include. A network with
.B enabled=false
is omitted from routes and policies. A community without enabled networks is
automatically disabled.
.SH DATABASE API
The Web UI and JSON API manage:
.TP
.B Network
prefix, description, status, community_ids and enabled.
.TP
.B Community
name, values, description and enabled.
.TP
.B BgpPeer
name, local ASN, neighbor address and neighbor ASN.
.PP
Deleting a Community also deletes all related Network records. The API is
unauthenticated; the optional user_id is stored in the audit log.
.SH DASHBOARD
The web page uses three left-aligned Windows-style tabs inside one centered
75%-wide block. The outer
.B .tabs
container owns the only
.B #cbd5e1
outline. The internal tab strip and panel area are layout-only and have no
frames. Inactive tabs keep a dark background and vertical separators; the
active white tab opens directly into the content area.
.TP
.B Сети
Network and community hierarchy plus the bulk network form. The hierarchy is
collapsed on first activation.
.TP
.B BIRD
Staged configuration, read-only generated files, Apply and BGP peer creation.
.TP
.B Система
A live BIRD peers block, configuration status, BIRD restart and reconfiguration
timestamps, a dedicated backup list and the audit log.
.PP
The page itself does not scroll. The outer tabs block fills the viewport height
between the header and its margins. On every tab the upper panel receives all
free vertical space while lower panels keep their natural height. Long content
scrolls only inside the panel that owns it.
.PP
The System tab order is BIRD peers, configuration status, backup and audit. The
backup panel shows used/limit slots and one row per archive with creation time,
restore and delete actions. Configuration status reads the actual BIRD restart
and reconfiguration timestamps from
.BR "birdc show status" .
.PP
The BIRD peers block is a read-only snapshot of
.BR "birdc show protocols all" .
Service protocols (any non-BGP type) are listed one per row with their up/down
state. BGP sessions are listed in a table with the columns Managed, Peer,
Neighbor, Neighbor AS, Local AS, State, Prefixes, Since and Last error. Managed
is
.B yes
when the protocol is in the panel peer list and
.B no
when it was added to bird.conf by hand. State holds two badges in one cell: the
channel state, and the BGP state taken from the BGP state line of
.BR "birdc show protocols all" ,
falling back to the summary State column. The second badge is shown only when it
differs from the channel state, because a session can be up while still not
exchanging routes. Colours are semantic - green for
.BR Established ,
amber for any other state - and hovering either badge names the birdc field the
value came from. Prefixes shows rx and tx; a dash means BIRD printed no route
counters for that channel, which is what a down session does. Last error is the
last column and stays empty while there is no error. A peer configured in the
panel but missing from the BIRD output is shown as not present in BIRD.
.PP
The block refreshes when the System tab activates, on the header refresh button,
and automatically every 30 seconds while the tab is active, the panel is
expanded and the page is visible.
.PP
The header has no user input. Web actions use the USERNAME value as their audit
identifier only; authentication and permissions are unchanged.
.PP
The active tab is stored in the URL hash as
.BR #/networks ,
.B #/bird
or
.BR #/system .
Data is loaded on first tab activation. The header refresh button reloads the
active tab and network counter.
.SH EDITOR
The editor exposes only
.IR networks.conf .
It does not display or modify routes.conf, core.conf or policy files. Preview
in split mode does not inject generated BIRD lines or ghost rows into the
editor.
.SH FIRST INSTALLATION
Install BIRD first. The package seeds a minimal live base and ensures that
.I /etc/bird/bird.conf
exists before starting bird.service. For a manual setup:
.PP
.nf
install bird3
systemctl enable --now bird
birdc show status
.fi
.PP
If bird.conf is an external configuration, the package can append one marked
stable include while preserving all existing content. A recognized legacy
manager block is migrated automatically. Every candidate is validated before
commit and the previous main file is backed up.
.SH HEALTH AND RELOAD
.nf
curl http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/health
birdc show status
.fi
.PP
A healthy response has status healthy. A failed BIRD reload returns degraded,
with reload_warning and reload_detail fields; the previous running BIRD
configuration is retained.
.SH EXAMPLE
Add a community and network through the API, then reconcile:
.PP
.nf
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/communities \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"name":"65500:102","enabled":true}'
.br
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/networks \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"prefix":"192.0.2.0/24","enabled":true}'
.br
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/config/generate
.fi
.PP
The generated policy is stored as policy_<community-id>.conf. The generated
bird-manager.conf entrypoint is updated automatically; bird.conf is unchanged.
.SH BACKUP
The backup API stores SQLite, staged/live files and the generated-file manifest,
with local rotation controlled by BIRD_BACKUP_RETENTION. The API returns every
archive, used and free slots. A selected archive can be restored with
.B POST /api/backup/<id>/restore
or deleted with
.BR "DELETE /api/backup/<id>" .
The compatibility endpoint
.B POST /api/backup/restore
restores the newest archive. IDs are strict
.B latest
or
.B YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-microseconds
identifiers; filesystem paths are rejected. The main config is archived for
offline recovery but is never restored over the installed file. Legacy archives
without bird-manager.conf synthesize the entrypoint during restore. Selected
archives pass
.B bird -p
before state is replaced, followed by
.BR "birdc configure" .
.SH UNINSTALL
.B apt remove bird-manager
asks whether to remove the database, staged files, backups, environment and
package-created live files. The default answer is No. On Yes, the managed block
in
.I /etc/bird/bird.conf
is replaced inline with the effective generated configuration. The candidate
must pass
.BR "bird -p" ,
and a running BIRD must accept
.BR "birdc configure" ,
before live files are removed. No persistent backup copy of bird.conf is
created. Unknown files in the live directory are preserved.
.PP
.B apt purge bird-manager
performs the same full cleanup without asking. The BIRD package, service and
bird group are not removed.
.SH SYSTEMD
The package installs
.IR bird-manager.service .
It runs as the dedicated bird-manager user with supplementary membership in
the bird group. The live directory is setgid so atomic replacements remain
readable by BIRD. Common operations:
.PP
.nf
systemctl status bird-manager
journalctl -u bird-manager -f
curl http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/health
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/bird-manager/.env
Runtime configuration (conffile).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/bird-manager
Application code.
.TP
.I /var/lib/bird-manager
SQLite database, staged files, backups and manifest.
.TP
.I /etc/bird/live
Generated and editable live files read by BIRD, including the generated
bird-manager.conf entrypoint.
.TP
.I /etc/bird/bird.conf
Operator-owned main configuration containing one stable manager include.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bird (8),
.BR birdc (8),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.SH AUTHORS
bird-manager maintainers.
